5.1.2.m:
"Everywhere your brilliant flashes, pure one, driven by the wind, / roam, O pure Agni;

crushing mightily, divine, roaming in groups of nine, / daringly breaking, they consume the forests."

"To you, O Agni, human clans pray, / the knower of the hotṛ's duties, the discriminator, the best bestower of treasures;

to him who is hidden in the secret cave, O fortunate one, visible to all, / loud-sounding, well-sacrificed, resting in clarified butter."

5.2.3.m:
Having cut off from the eastern half of the middle [cake] and from the eastern half [of the other], [the adhvaryu commands]: “Recite the invitatory verse for Indra connected with Rathantara.”

“Towards you, O hero, we call aloud, / like unmilked milk cows;

The ruler of this moving world, who sees the sun, / the ruler, om.”

“Recite the invitatory verse for Indra connected with Bṛhat.”

“You indeed we invoke, / we, the singers, for the winning of the prize;

You, O Indra, the true lord, men [invoke] among the Vṛtras; / you, om!”

“Recite the offering verse for Indra connected with Rathantara.” / “We who sacrifice to Indra connected with Rathantara…”

“…with steed in the contests. / Towards you, O hero, we call aloud, / like unmilked milk cows;

The ruler of this moving world, who sees the sun, / the ruler, O Indra, of the stationary; vaṣaṭ!”

5.2.6.m:
"Stay, O Maruts, the departing courser, / swift as thought, the bull, beautifully formed;

By whom this fierce [wind] goes forth released, / him do you, O Aśvins, invest for well-being."

With this, he accompanies the rain-bringing wind from the north with the mantra.
